<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>

<!--
   Quantum ESPRESSO (pw.x) code registration, PHASE 1 reduced-scope
   first cut.  See docs/QE_ROADMAP.md for the full plan; this file
   implements its section 5 "Phase 1".  Modeled on MOPAC.edml (issue
   #86) and ORCA.edml (issue #38) as the two most recent worked
   examples, and on MetaDyn.edml as the only existing plane-wave-only
   code registration.

   QE IS A PLANE-WAVE code, which makes this file structurally like the
   nwpw path rather than like any Gaussian-basis code:

     * There is NO <GaussianBasisSetRules> block and NO
       <BasisTranslationScript>.  A plane-wave basis is defined by a
       kinetic-energy cutoff (ecutwfc), which is a field in the Theory
       Details dialog, not a library selection.  The single <Theory>
       below carries needsBasis="false", the pre-existing ECCE
       mechanism for this (NWChem.edml's category="NWPW" theories and
       MOPAC.edml's category="SE" theories both use it):
       CalcEd::updateBasisWidgets() greys out the Basis Set Tool, and
       ESInputController's write_gbsconfig()/write_setup() skip the
       .basis file and the BasisSet.Coordinates key entirely, so ai.qe
       is never handed a -b argument.  CodeFactory::getBasisCodes()
       likewise filters QE out of the Basis Set Tool's code list
       because <GaussianBasisSetRules> is absent.  No C++ change was
       needed for any of this.

     * PERIODICITY IS MANDATORY, unlike for every other registered
       code.  pw.x cannot run without a unit cell, so a structure with
       no LatticeDef cannot produce a valid deck.  ai.qe refuses
       loudly (naming the Builder's "Periodic Builder" tool panel)
       rather than emitting a deck pw.x would reject with a cryptic
       namelist error.  The cell comes from the fragment ONLY, via the
       .frag's lattice_vectors: section; nwpw's second, unsynchronized
       set of cell fields in its own theory dialog is deliberately not
       repeated here.

     * There is no <Importer> (a QuantumESPRESSO.expt post-hoc output
       importer for externally-run jobs is not written yet), so QE
       simply does not appear in CodeFactory::getImportCodes(), which
       is correct behavior rather than an error.  ORCA.edml declares an
       <Importer>ORCA.expt</Importer> that does not exist on disk;
       that is not copied here.

   THE INPUT FILE NAME IS FREE, unlike MOPAC's.  pw.x takes its deck
   with "-in <file>" and writes its report to stdout, so it has no
   opinion about either name and the distinctive extensions the
   new-code checklist calls for are simply available:  .pwin / .pwout,
   with matching AddType lines in
   packaging/dataserver/httpd.conf.ecce.  Generic .in/.out would fall
   through to Apache's DefaultType text/plain and break the
   mimetype-filtered primary-input lookup in a way that looks exactly
   like a save failure.

   NO VIBRATION OR GEOVIB RUNTYPE, and no relax/vc-relax/nscf/bands,
   deliberately.  Vibrational frequencies in QE need a THREE-executable
   chain, pw.x then ph.x then dynmat.x, sharing prefix/outdir: that is
   the one part of QE that does not fit ECCE's "one calculation, one
   input, one binary, one output" model (see docs/QE_ROADMAP.md
   section 3.2), and the Vibrational Frequencies panel it would feed is
   itself currently broken under wx3.2/GTK3 (issue #81).  Shipping
   without it has precedent: MOPAC shipped with no transition-state
   runtype.  The other calculation modes are phases 3 to 5 of the
   roadmap.

   Verified end to end against the real installed Quantum ESPRESSO 6.7
   (Debian quantum-espresso 6.7-3+b1, /usr/bin/pw.x) on 2026-09-21:
   decks generated by ai.qe from real .param/.frag pairs were run to
   completion ("JOB DONE.") for bulk silicon (Monkhorst-Pack grid,
   fixed occupations), water in a box (gamma point, two species), fcc
   aluminium (metallic smearing) and triplet O2 (nspin = 2,
   tot_magnetization).  pw.x echoes any unrecognized namelist variable
   as a fatal error, so no emitted keyword can pass silently.
-->

<appdescriptor>

<!--
  Enables Geometry and Partial Charge sub dialogs if runtype is
  appropriate.  QE does support fixed atoms (the if_pos flags on
  ATOMIC_POSITIONS) but only for a relax run, which phase 1 does not
  offer, and ai.qe does not write them, so this is left false rather
  than advertising a capability the input generator drops.
-->
<SupportsConstraints>false</SupportsConstraints>


<!-- One plane-wave theory, one runtype.  "PW" is the plane-wave
     pseudopotential method itself; the exchange-correlation functional
     is NOT part of the theory name because for a pseudopotential code
     it is a property of the pseudopotentials (the packaged Debian SSSP
     library is entirely PBE).  The Theory Details dialog offers an
     input_dft override for the rare case where that is wanted.

     Energy maps to calculation='scf'.  Forces and the stress tensor
     are available on an scf run (tprnfor / tstress) and are exposed as
     Runtype Details checkboxes, so a separate Gradient runtype would
     add nothing in phase 1. -->
<Editor theorydialog="qetheory.py" runtypedialog="qeruntype.py">
  <Theory category="PW" name="PW" needsBasis="false">
    <runtype>Energy</runtype>
  </Theory>

  <TheorySummary topLabel="Plane Waves-">
    <item key="ES.Theory.PW.EcutWfc" label="Wavefn. Cutoff (Ry)"></item>
  </TheorySummary>
  <TheorySummary>
    <item key="ES.Theory.PW.Occupations" label="Occupations"></item>
  </TheorySummary>
  <TheorySummary>
    <item key="ES.Theory.PW.KPointScheme" label="k-points"></item>
  </TheorySummary>
  <TheorySummary>
    <item key="ES.Theory.PW.ConvergenceEnergy" label="SCF Convergence"></item>
  </TheorySummary>

  <RuntypeSummary topLabel="Properties-">
    <item key="ES.Runtype.PW.PrintForces" label="Forces"></item>
  </RuntypeSummary>
  <RuntypeSummary>
    <item key="ES.Runtype.PW.PrintStress" label="Stress"></item>
  </RuntypeSummary>
</Editor>


<!-- Raw File Naming Conventions.  See the header comment for why the
     extensions are free to be distinctive here (pw.x names neither
     file itself).  verifypattern is "Program PWSCF" and deliberately
     carries NO version number: the banner reads
     "Program PWSCF v.6.7MaX starts on ...", so anchoring on the
     version would break on every QE upgrade.  MOPAC could get away
     with "MOPAC v" only because there the name and the "v" are
     adjacent.

     comment/commentstring are declared for consistency with the other
     descriptors, but note two things: no C++ in this tree reads either
     attribute (JCode.C parses only type, mimetype and verifypattern),
     and QE accepts "!" comments inside a NAMELIST but NOT inside a
     card such as ATOMIC_SPECIES, where a "!" line is a fatal
     card_atomic_species read error (verified against the real 6.7
     binary). -->
<DataFiles>
  <Input  type="primary" mimetype="chemical/x-qe-input" comment="true" commentstring="!">qe.pwin</Input>
  <Output type="primary" mimetype="chemical/x-qe-output">qe.pwout</Output>
  <Output type="parse" verifypattern="Program PWSCF" mimetype="chemical/x-qe-output">qe.pwout</Output>
</DataFiles>

<!-- Names of files required for code integration.  These files are
     assumed to be in the ecce path as managed by the ecce_env script.
     All of these exist under scripts/parsers.  <LaunchPreprocessor> is
     required unconditionally: Launch::postProcessInput() shells out to
     JCode::launchPPScript() with no empty-string check, so a code
     without one fails launch with a bare "-p postParams" command name.
     QE genuinely needs one anyway, to rewrite &CONTROL outdir to the
     scratch directory Launch actually allocated. -->
<IntegrationFiles>
  <Template>qe.tpl</Template>
  <InputGenerator>ai.qe</InputGenerator>
  <ParseSpecification>qe.desc</ParseSpecification>
  <LaunchPreprocessor>qe.launchpp</LaunchPreprocessor>
</IntegrationFiles>

</appdescriptor>
